.\" bluetoothd(8) — NextBSD Bluetooth daemon.
.\"
.\" NextBSD-original. There is no upstream page to vendor: Apple's bluetoothd
.\" is closed source and undocumented.
.\"
.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt BLUETOOTHD 8
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm bluetoothd
.Nd Bluetooth daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
owns Bluetooth on NextBSD.
It is started by
.Xr launchd 8
as
.Ql org.nextbsd.bluetoothd ,
serves the Mach service
.Ql org.nextbsd.bluetooth ,
and is not intended to be run by hand.
Use
.Xr bluetooth 8
to talk to it.
.Pp
.Nm
and
.Xr bluetooth 8
are the only Bluetooth programs on the system.
The kernel stack, loaded as kexts, carries HCI and L2CAP; everything above it
lives in
.Nm :
controller bring-up and power, discovery, pairing for Classic (BR/EDR) and LE
with one pairing store, the SDP server and client, the HID host and the audio
roles.
It runs as root from boot, so a paired keyboard works at the login window.
.Pp
Bluetooth is on by default.
A controller is powered on when it arrives unless the persisted
.Ql ControllerPowerState
is 0, and paired devices reconnect without user action.
.Sh CONTROLLERS
.Pa NgUbt.kext
publishes each controller in the IORegistry as an
.Ql IOBluetoothHostController
entry once its netgraph node exists, and retires the entry when the controller
goes away.
.Nm
follows that class with
.Fn IOServiceAddMatchingNotification ,
first-match and terminated, as
.Xr wland 8
follows
.Ql IO80211Controller .
A controller that is already present when
.Nm
starts is delivered as an arrival.
.Pp
With no controller,
.Nm
logs one line, blocks with no timer and loads no kexts: the upper kernel stack
is loaded when the first controller arrives.
On a kernel without
.Pa /dev/ioregistry
no controller can be seen;
.Nm
stays up to answer
.Xr bluetooth 8
and reports itself as blind.
.Sh ARCHITECTURE
.Bd -literal -offset indent
NgUbt.kext ──IOBluetoothHostController──▶ bluetoothd
bluetoothd ──raw HCI / L2CAP sockets──▶ kernel stack
           ──SCPreferences────────────▶ pairing store + policy
           ──SCDynamicStore───────────▶ State:/Bluetooth
           ──MIG org.nextbsd.bluetooth──▶ bluetooth(8) ──▶ Gershwin
.Ed
.Pp
Inside, a policy layer (controllers, the device list, pairing prompts, the
store, publication, the Mach service) sits above two protocol engines, Classic
and LE, behind one interface.
The policy layer never needs to know which transport a device uses: addresses
are typed (public or random), one prompt shape covers legacy PIN, Secure Simple
Pairing and LE pairing, and one bond record holds both engines' keys.
.Sh PAIRING PROMPTS
Nothing above the Mach line can receive a Mach message, so
.Nm
never calls a client back.
When pairing needs the user,
.Nm
parks the request and a client fetches and answers it
.Pq the pull model .
A prompt is one of: confirm (just works), numeric comparison, passkey entry,
passkey display, or a legacy PIN.
.Sh PAIRING STORE
Two files under
.Pa /Local/Library/Preferences/SystemConfiguration ,
both written through SCPreferences (temporary file and rename on every
commit):
.Bl -tag -width "org.nextbsd.Bluetooth.keys.plist"
.It Pa org.nextbsd.Bluetooth.plist
Mode 0644.
Policy and device metadata, readable by tools:
.Bl -tag -width "ControllerPowerState" -compact
.It Ql ControllerPowerState
1 on, 0 off; missing means on.
.It Ql DiscoverableState
Inquiry scan at power-up; default off.
.It Ql AutoConnect
Reconnect paired devices at power-up; default on.
.It Ql PairedDevices
One dictionary per device, keyed by its address
.Pq Ql aa-bb-cc-dd-ee-ff :
.Ql Name , AddressType , Transports , ClassOfDevice , Appearance , PnPID ,
.Ql Profiles , HIDDescriptor , AutoConnect , Trusted , Paired ,
and
.Ql LastConnected .
The cached HID descriptor lets a paired keyboard reconnect with no service
query.
.El
.It Pa org.nextbsd.Bluetooth.keys.plist
Mode 0600.
The secrets:
.Ql LinkKeys
(Classic),
.Ql LongTermKeys ,
.Ql IdentityResolvingKeys
and
.Ql SignatureKeys
(LE), keyed by the same address, in HCI byte order.
.El
.Pp
.Sy Keys are stored in plaintext .
NextBSD has no keychain; this is the same debt
.Xr wland 8
carries for passphrases.
The keys have a file of their own, mode 0600 and root-owned, so that when a
keychain lands it replaces that one file.
Key bytes are never written to the log.
.Pp
A missing or corrupt file reads as empty;
.Nm
never fails to start because of its store.
.Sh DYNAMIC STORE
.Bl -tag -width "State:/Bluetooth/Adapter/<node>"
.It Ql State:/Bluetooth
The daemon:
.Ql State
.Po
.Ql Idle ,
.Ql Active
or
.Ql NoIORegistry
.Pc ,
.Ql Adapters ,
.Ql PairedDevices ,
and the policy keys above.
.It Ql State:/Bluetooth/Adapter/<node>
One controller.
.It Ql State:/Bluetooth/Device/<addr>
One paired or connected device.
.El
.Pp
Lists do not go in the store; they are served over the Mach service.
.Xr scutil 8
shows these keys.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/Local/Library/Preferences/SystemConfiguration/" -compact
.It Pa /usr/sbin/bluetoothd
The daemon.
.It Pa /dev/ioregistry
The in-kernel IORegistry, where controllers appear as
.Ql IOBluetoothHostController .
.It Pa /System/Library/LaunchDaemons/org.nextbsd.bluetoothd.plist
.Xr launchd 8
job.
.It Pa /var/log/bluetoothd.stderr
Log.
.It Pa /Local/Library/Preferences/SystemConfiguration/
.Pa org.nextbsd.Bluetooth.plist
(policy and paired devices) and
.Pa org.nextbsd.Bluetooth.keys.plist
(plaintext keys).
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bluetooth 8 ,
.Xr configd 8 ,
.Xr ioreg 8 ,
.Xr launchd 8 ,
.Xr scutil 8 ,
.Xr wland 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in NextBSD in 2026.
It is a NextBSD original; Apple has never published its Bluetooth source.
